#module "containers"
'' std/containers: hash -- the hash contract for containers.Map and containers.Set
''
'' A key type K is hashable if 'HashOf( k )' resolves and 'k = k'
'' compares.  Overloads are supplied here for every built-in type; a user type is
'' made hashable by extending Hashable and giving it a HashOf( ) member -- see
'' Hashable at the end of this file:
''
''     type MyKey extends containers.Hashable
''         as string name
''         as long id
''         declare function HashOf( ) as ulongint override
''     end type
''     function MyKey.HashOf( ) as ulongint
''         return containers.HashOf( name ) xor culngint( id )
''     end function
''
'' THE INVARIANT, and getting it wrong produces a container that loses data:
''
''     EQUAL KEYS MUST PRODUCE EQUAL HASH CODES.
''
'' The converse is not required -- two different keys may collide, and the
'' containers handle that.
''
'' Why an overloaded function rather than a 'HashCode( )' member: a member cannot
'' be added to 'long' or 'string', and a
'' generic body cannot branch on its type parameter.  'typeof( T )' inside a
'' generic sees the type parameter, not what it is bound to --
''
''     type Alias1 as long
''     #if typeof( Alias1 ) = typeof( long )   '' matches
''     #if typeof( T ) = typeof( long )        '' does NOT match, T bound to long
''
'' -- measured, not assumed.  So the dispatch has to be ordinary overload
'' resolution, which happens at the instantiation site where every overload is
'' in scope.
''
'' A key type with no matching overload fails at the instantiation site naming
'' HashOf, which is the usual unconstrained-generics diagnostic.
''
'' EVERY OVERLOAD BELOW IS PUBLIC, defined once: std/containers is one module. As fb/hash.bi they
'' were 'PRIVATE', as HashBytes and HashInt were: that was header-only code with no .bas behind
'' it, so a public definition was emitted into every module that included the header -- and two
'' modules in one program then failed to link with ~18 'multiple definition of FB::HASHOF(...)'
'' errors, naming a standard-library function and neither of the two files
'' responsible.  fb/map.bi and fb/set.bi included that header, so without
'' 'private' a Map or a Set could be used from only ONE module per program.
'' Same rule as fb/decimal.bi and fb/chrono.bi followed.
''



'' FNV-1a, 64-bit.  Small, adequate, no dependencies and unremarkable -- nothing
'' in the containers depends on the choice.
public const FNV_OFFSET_BASIS as ulongint = 14695981039346656037ull
public const FNV_PRIME        as ulongint = 1099511628211ull

public function HashBytes _
    ( _
        byval p as const ubyte ptr, _
        byval n as uinteger _
    ) as ulongint

    dim as ulongint h = FNV_OFFSET_BASIS

    '' n is UNSIGNED, so 'to n-1' with n = 0 counts to 4294967295 and walks off
    '' the end.  An empty literal reaches here through the ZSTRING PTR overload,
    '' which a "" argument prefers over the STRING one, so the empty case is not
    '' hypothetical -- it segfaulted on the first run.
    if( (p = nullptr) orelse (n = 0) ) then
        return h
    end if

    for i as uinteger = 0 to n-1
        h xor= p[i]
        h *= FNV_PRIME
    next

    function = h
end function

'' 2^64 / phi, odd.  Map and Set take a key's home slot as the top bits of
'' 'HashOf( k ) * CONTAINERS_FIB_MULT' (Fibonacci hashing, Map.HomeOf), so the
'' containers do their own final mixing and a hash need not mix its low bits.
const CONTAINERS_FIB_MULT as ulongint = 11400714819323198485ull

'' Integer mixing (splitmix64's finaliser).  The integer HashOf overloads do
'' not call it -- see below -- but it is the tool for COMBINING hashes in
'' a user type's HashOf( ), where a weak 'a xor ( b shl 1 )' of two identity
'' hashes collides on small values.
public function HashInt( byval v as ulongint ) as ulongint
    dim as ulongint h = v
    h xor= h shr 30
    h *= 13787848793156543929ull
    h xor= h shr 27
    h *= 10723151780598845931ull
    h xor= h shr 31
    function = h
end function

'' ---------------------------------------------------------------- integers
''
'' THE IDENTITY, zero-extended.  Map and Set mix in HomeOf, and Fibonacci hashing
'' spreads a dense run of small integers into nearly distinct slots, where a
'' random-looking mix (splitmix, as these were) collides at the load factor's
'' rate.  Measured on the Benchmarks Game k-nucleotide port: 8.02 s -> 7.18 s.

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as byte ) as ulongint
    function = culngint( cast( ubyte, v ) )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as ubyte ) as ulongint
    function = culngint( v )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as short ) as ulongint
    function = culngint( cast( ushort, v ) )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as ushort ) as ulongint
    function = culngint( v )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as long ) as ulongint
    function = culngint( cast( ulong, v ) )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as ulong ) as ulongint
    function = culngint( v )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as integer ) as ulongint
    function = culngint( cast( uinteger, v ) )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as uinteger ) as ulongint
    function = culngint( v )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as longint ) as ulongint
    function = cast( ulongint, v )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as ulongint ) as ulongint
    function = v
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as boolean ) as ulongint
    function = iif( v, 1ull, 0ull )
end function

'' ------------------------------------------------------------------ floats
''
'' Hashed by their bit pattern, with one correction: +0.0 and -0.0 compare EQUAL
'' and have different bit patterns, so -0.0 is normalised.  Without this a
'' dictionary would lose an entry stored under -0.0 and looked up under 0.0.
''
'' NaN is left alone.  NaN <> NaN, so a NaN key can never be found again by
'' comparison whatever it hashes to; that is a property of the value, not of
'' this function.

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as single ) as ulongint
    dim as single f = v
    if( f = 0.0f ) then
        f = 0.0f
    end if
    function = HashInt( culngint( *cptr( ulong ptr, @f ) ) )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as double ) as ulongint
    dim as double d = v
    if( d = 0.0 ) then
        d = 0.0
    end if
    function = HashInt( *cptr( ulongint ptr, @d ) )
end function

'' ---------------------------------------------------------------- pointers

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as any ptr ) as ulongint
    function = HashInt( cast( ulongint, cast( uinteger, v ) ) )
end function

'' ----------------------------------------------------------------- strings
''
'' By CONTENT, not by address, because two strings with the same characters
'' compare equal and therefore must hash equal.

public function HashOf overload ( byref v as const string ) as ulongint
    if( len( v ) = 0 ) then
        return FNV_OFFSET_BASIS
    end if
    function = HashBytes( cptr( const ubyte ptr, strptr( v ) ), len( v ) )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as const zstring ptr ) as ulongint
    if( v = nullptr ) then
        return FNV_OFFSET_BASIS
    end if
    function = HashBytes( cptr( const ubyte ptr, v ), len( *v ) )
end function

public function HashOf overload ( byval v as const wstring ptr ) as ulongint
    if( v = nullptr ) then
        return FNV_OFFSET_BASIS
    end if
    function = HashBytes( cptr( const ubyte ptr, v ), len( *v ) * sizeof( wstring ) )
end function

'' USTRING.  Two code units per character on every host and target, so unlike the
'' WSTRING overload above this one's byte count is not host-dependent -- LEN( u )
'' counts CODE UNITS and each is exactly 2 bytes.  STRPTR on a var-len ustring
'' yields the DESCRIPTOR'S DATA pointer, not the descriptor's address.
''
'' Deliberately NOT expected to agree with HashOf( wstring ptr ) for the same
'' text: it does on Windows (wstring is 2 bytes) and cannot on Linux (4 bytes).
'' Nothing needs it to -- a container's key type is one type, and any conversion
'' to it happens before HashOf is reached.
public function HashOf overload ( byref v as const ustring ) as ulongint
    if( len( v ) = 0 ) then
        return FNV_OFFSET_BASIS
    end if
    function = HashBytes( cptr( const ubyte ptr, strptr( v ) ), len( v ) * 2 )
end function


'' A USER TYPE'S HASH: its own HashOf( ) member, through the abstract base Hashable. As
'' fb/hash.bi a user type was made hashable by re-opening namespace FB and adding a HashOf
'' overload; a module's namespace cannot be re-opened, and an overload declared anywhere else
'' is invisible to Map's and Set's bodies, which are the module's. So a key type carries its
'' hash with it:
''
''     type PairKey extends containers.Hashable
''         as long a, b
''         declare function HashOf( ) as ulongint override
''     end type
''     function PairKey.HashOf( ) as ulongint
''         return containers.HashInt( containers.HashOf( a ) ) xor containers.HashOf( b )
''     end function
''
'' Mix one side with HashInt: the integer hashes are the identity, so a bare
'' 'HashOf( a ) xor ( HashOf( b ) shl 1 )' maps (2, 0) and (0, 1) to one code.
''
'' NOT A GENERIC FALLBACK, HashOf( of K )( k ) calling k.HashOf( ): a generic candidate that
'' matches exactly is preferred to an overload needing a conversion (measured, fbc-modern), so
'' it would capture the enum and pointer keys the long and 'any ptr' overloads serve today.
public type Hashable extends object
    declare abstract function HashOf( ) as ulongint
end type

public function HashOf overload ( byref k as Hashable ) as ulongint
    return k.HashOf( )
end function
